.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.\" dpt(1) - Diagnostic Ports Time
.TH DPT 1 "2026-11-25" "dpt" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dpt \- Diagnostic Ports Time: is this address working as a network peer, and what is on the way to it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dpt
.RI [ address | name ]
.RB [ \-tdp ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B dpt dhcp
.RB [ \-td ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B dpt info
.br
.B dpt smtp
.IR host " [" "test user " [ password "]] [" "from address" "] [" "to address" ]
.br
.B dpt smtp prompt
.br
.B dpt sweep
.RB [ ports ]
.RI [ network ]
.br
.B dpt ports
.IR host " [" ports ]
.br
.B dpt connect
.IR "host port" " [" tls | notls ]
.br
.B dpt trace
.I host
.br
.B dpt whois
.IR what " [" server ]
.RB [ both ]
.br
.B dpt rdap
.IR what " [" server ]
.br
.B dpt time
.RI [ host ]
.br
.B dpt time native
.RB [ sync " [" fix ]]
.br
.B dpt outside\-ip
.br
.B dpt install
|
.B dpt upgrade
.br
.B dpt
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dpt
tests an address the way a network engineer would: ping, DNS with the address
as the resolver, and NTP time with this machine's clock checked against
reference servers; then what the address offers.
It also finds every DHCP server on the segment, checks mail servers, sweeps a
network, tries ports, connects as telnet would, traces the route, says who holds
an address or a domain (whois and RDAP), and runs the system's own time tools.
With no arguments it is an interactive shell that works as
.BR nslookup (1)
does, with the commands below added.
.PP
It is one program for Linux, macOS and Windows, on Intel and ARM.
Options are written the way the system writes them:
.B \-c 3 \-w 2
on Linux and macOS
.RB ( "\-\-count 3" ,
.BR \-\-timeout=2s ),
.B /c:3 /w:2
on Windows.
.SH TESTS
The tests are letters written together (none: all three):
.TP
.B t
time: an NTP query of the address, compared with time.google.com and
time.windows.com, and this machine's clock checked against them
(OK within 50 ms, WARN within 1 s, FAIL beyond, or unsynchronised).
.TP
.B d
DNS: lookups with the address as the resolver: recursion, EDNS0, DNSSEC
validation, DNS over TCP.
.TP
.B p
ping: ICMP echo.
.PP
When all three run, FEATURES lists what the address offers of what dpt tries:
icmp, dns/udp, dns/tcp, recursion, edns0, dnssec, dot(853), ntp, whois(43),
rdap (HTTPS, asked under the name when one is given), ldap(389) and ldaps(636).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI dhcp
Sends one DHCPDISCOVER and shows every offer, rogue servers too, without taking
a lease;
.B t
and
.B d
then test the NTP and DNS servers the offers name.
It needs root (Linux: or CAP_NET_RAW) or an Administrator prompt; typed at a
terminal, dpt asks for it itself (sudo, or UAC on Windows).
.TP
.B info
This machine: each interface's address, gateway and DHCP lease; the DNS and NTP
servers it uses; its clock against Google, Microsoft, AWS, Apple and NIST; the
addresses the internet sees (asks https://ifconfig.me/ip, over IPv4 and IPv6).
.TP
.BI smtp " host"
Ports 25, 465, 587, 2525 and 8025: the greeting, STARTTLS or TLS, the
certificate, AUTH, why a port fails, a firewall in the way; then what mail can
and cannot do from here.
.BI test " user"
logs in (the password is asked for when not given);
.BI to " address"
then sends a test message.
.B smtp prompt
asks a line at a time, offering the last answers.
.TP
.BR sweep " [" ports "] [\fInetwork\fR]"
Pings every address of this network, or of
.I network
(10.0.0.0/24; an address: its /24; at most /22), 25 at a time;
.B ports
then tries the common ports of each that answers.
.TP
.BI ports " host" " \fR[\fIports\fR]"
The 66 common TCP ports (whois 43, LDAP 389 and 636, mail on 2525 and 8025,
HTTPS on 1443 ... 14443 among them) and 11 UDP services: DNS, TFTP, QUIC, NTP,
NetBIOS, SNMP, RADIUS (1812, accounting 1813), SSDP and mDNS.
Each UDP port is sent a real, harmless request of its own; silence is
"no answer": open and quiet, or dropped by a firewall.
A RADIUS server that checks its shared secret (dpt guesses none) stays silent:
only a closed RADIUS port is told apart.
.I ports
is a list: 587,2525,8000\-8010,53/udp.
.TP
.BI connect " host port" " \fR[\fBtls\fR|\fBnotls\fR]"
Connects as telnet would: how long it took, the TLS and certificate, what the
server says first (SSH, SMTP, FTP, a switch's login prompt...); then, at a
terminal, what is typed goes to the server a line at a time.
.TP
.BI trace " host"
The routers on the way, each hop asked 3 times at once, with times and loss.
.TP
.BI whois " what" " \fR[\fIserver\fR] [\fBboth\fR]"
Who holds an address, a network, an AS number or a domain
(1.1.1.1, 2001:db8::/32, AS13335, example.com), by whois on port 43:
IANA, the registry it refers to, then one referral from there.
It shows a summary (holder, network or AS, abuse contact, dates; for a domain
its registrar, status, name servers, DNSSEC) read from the text, then the
registry's text itself, and says whether RDAP has it too.
.I server
asks that whois server only, as
.B whois \-h
does.
.B both
asks RDAP too, and shows the two answers one after the other.
.TP
.BI rdap " what" " \fR[\fIserver\fR]"
The same by RDAP: IANA's lists name the registry's RDAP server, and its JSON
answer is read the same way from every registry.
A domain's registrar is asked after its registry (it holds the registrant).
It says whether whois has it too, and when there is no RDAP server for it
(some country domains: .de, .io, .jp).
.I server
is an RDAP server, https://....
.B \-\-verbose
adds every contact and remark;
.B \-\-json
gives the summary and the registry's answer itself.
.IP
For both: a private or documentation address is named as such, and no registry
is asked.
.TP
.BI time " \fR[\fIhost\fR]"
This machine's clock against the reference servers (time.google.com,
time.windows.com; \fB\-\-ntp\fR), and, given a host, its NTP time beside them.
.TP
.B time native \fR[\fBsync\fR [\fBfix\fR]]
Runs the system's own time tools, each command shown and run when Enter is
pressed (Ctrl\-C stops):
.B w32tm /query /status
and
.B w32tm /stripchart
on Windows,
.B sntp
on macOS,
.BR chronyc ,
.B timedatectl
or
.B ntpq
on Linux; then dpt measures the clock itself against time.cloudflare.com,
time.google.com and time.windows.com.
.IP
.B sync
resyncs now (as Administrator or root), measured before and after.
.IP
.B sync fix
sets the system to keep its clock in sync: on Windows, Windows Time starts with
Windows and syncs with the three servers every hour; on macOS, network time on;
on Linux, the installed time service on.
It says first that you should be the system admin, and how to put things back,
and waits for Enter; from a script it never runs.
On a Windows PC joined to a domain it refuses and changes nothing: such a PC
must take its time from a domain controller.
.TP
.B outside\-ip
The address the internet sees.
.TP
.B install
Puts this dpt where the system finds it (/usr/local/bin; Windows:
C:\eProgram Files\edpt, added to PATH), and this page in
/usr/local/share/man/man1.
.TP
.B upgrade
Downloads the newest release, checks it against the release's SHA256SUMS, and
installs it; never an older one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-count " \fIn\fR"
Pings and DNS lookups per run (5).
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-timeout " \fIs\fR"
How long to wait for each answer, seconds or a duration such as 500ms (2).
.TP
.BI \-\-names " a,b,c"
Names to resolve.
.TP
.BI \-\-ntp " a,b"
Reference NTP servers.
.TP
.B \-\-all
Test every address a name resolves to.
.TP
.BI \-\-iface " name"
dhcp, info, sweep: the interface (else the Ethernet one with the LAN gateway).
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " s"
dhcp: how long to collect offers (5).
.TP
.B \-\-select
dhcp, info, sweep: list the interfaces and choose one.
.TP
.B \-\-random\-mac
dhcp: probe with a random MAC.
.TP
.BR \-4 ", " \-6
IPv4 only, IPv6 only.
.TP
.B \-\-json
One JSON document.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
More: a line per echo and per lookup; every RDAP contact and remark.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
No colour (as does NO_COLOR in the environment).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
The help.
.TP
.B \-\-version
The version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All OK.
.TP
.B 1
A test failed (or nothing was found).
.TP
.B 2
Minimum execution level not met: a privilege or the network dpt needs.
.TP
.B 3
Bad usage.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.dpt
The smtp prompt's last answers, the password among them: keep it private
(Windows: %APPDATA%\edpt\e.dpt).
.TP
.I ~/.dpt_history
The shell's history (Windows: %APPDATA%\edpt\ehistory).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B dpt 1.1.1.1
Ping, DNS and time of 1.1.1.1, and its features.
.TP
.B sudo dpt dhcp
Every DHCP server on the segment.
.TP
.B dpt ports mail.example.com 587,2525,8025
Three ports.
.TP
.B dpt whois 8.8.8.8 both
Who holds 8.8.8.8, by whois and by RDAP.
.TP
.B dpt rdap example.com
A domain's registry and registrar, by RDAP.
.TP
.B dpt time native sync
Resync this clock, and see it before and after.
.SH SEE ALSO
